---
import {goalForSport, NEW_TAB_NOTICE, PATCHES, type RaceEvent, recordingsOf, stravaActivityUrl} from "../lib/constants";
import {patchDateSegments, type PatchState} from "../lib/projection";
import {iconClass} from "../lib/icons";

interface Props {
    event: RaceEvent;
    /** Derived from the calendar by `patchState`, never stored. See projection.ts. */
    state: PatchState;
}

const {event, state} = Astro.props;

/*
 * ONE BIB. Two treatments of it, and the distance is the only thing drawn large.
 *
 * The sport's icon and name come from the GOAL that owns the sport, never from a
 * lookup beside EVENTS — `goalForSport` in constants.ts has the argument, and the
 * short version is that uno.config.ts safelists icon classes by reading GOALS and
 * has no reason to read EVENTS. A second lookup ships a class UnoCSS never generated
 * a rule for, which renders as a mask box at zero size: correct markup, no icon,
 * nothing red.
 *
 * THE FRACTION IS SPLIT OUT so it can be set small. A race distance is measured to
 * two decimals and the hundredths are real — 140.49 is not 140 — but at the size
 * this number is drawn they would dominate the digits that carry the meaning. The
 * text content is unbroken either way, so a screen reader still hears "140.49".
 *
 * `toFixed(2)` IS WHAT MAKES THAT A COLUMN OF ONE KIND OF NUMBER, and the races on the
 * wall print in THREE different shapes without it: 22.45, 130.03 and 160.56
 * already carry two places, 158.10, 21.10 and 42.20 lose their trailing zero to `158.1`,
 * `21.1` and `42.2`, and 1022.00 loses the point entirely to `1022`. (An earlier revision
 * of this comment claimed all but the tour were already two-place — worth the correction
 * because it is the SINGLE-place ones that make the case: a lone bare integer reads as an
 * approximation, and a mix of one and two places reads as inconsistent precision. Which
 * races fall in which group moves with the data, so re-read the array rather than this
 * list.) The bibs do not form a column — each sits in its own grid
 * cell at its own width — so this is not about digit alignment; it is that every bib
 * should claim the same precision about its distance.
 *
 * THE STATE IS SAID IN WORDS AS WELL AS IN THE TREATMENT. The outline carries it
 * visually and the tag carries it in text, which is not redundancy to be tidied
 * away — SC 1.4.1 does not accept a visual treatment as the only carrier of
 * information. A finished bib needs no tag because it is the unmarked case, and the
 * wall's lede gives it its name — a Finisher Patch.
 *
 * THE MIRROR-IMAGE TAG WAS CONSIDERED AND REJECTED when the earned bib was named,
 * which is the obvious next move and the wrong one. You mark the exception, not the
 * norm: EVENTS is the owner's whole racing history now, so the earned bib is the
 * MAJORITY of a full wall, and a label repeated on nine bibs in ten stops being
 * information. `booked_label` earns its place precisely because the outline is the
 * minority — the maintainer's call, taken with both options drawn.
 *
 * THE WHOLE BIB IS THE LINK WHERE THERE IS ONE, and the tag it renders as is therefore
 * dynamic. Safe, because every selector involved is class-based — `.bib`, `.bib--booked`,
 * `.bib-meta` — as are `structuralSelector` and the colour model in patch-wall.test.ts;
 * an element-name selector here would have made this a rewrite. `container-type` stays on
 * whichever element wears `.bib`, or the hero's `22cqi` loses its container.
 *
 * IT IS CONDITIONAL ON THE RECORDINGS, NOT ON THE STATE, because `recordings` is optional
 * and a finished race without one must still render as an ordinary finished bib — the rule
 * above `EVENTS` in constants.ts, and what makes filling in a back catalogue a data edit.
 * Whether any race on the wall is in that state right now is not the point and changes with
 * the data; this used to name one that has since been recorded. The fixtures in
 * patch-wall.test.ts hold the case either way.
 *
 * AND IT IS CONDITIONAL ON HOW MANY. **A bib is the link when there is one place to go;
 * when there is more than one, the bib HOLDS the links instead.** A race recorded in parts
 * has no single honest destination — Strava cannot merge two activities, so there is no URL
 * that is the whole race — and anchors do not nest, so the bib cannot both be a link and
 * contain them. The usual escape, a stretched `::before` over the card, is unavailable here:
 * patch-wall.test.ts refuses any rule reaching a bib through a pseudo-element, by
 * construction.
 *
 * THE ALTERNATIVE WAS TO DELEGATE ON EVERY BIB, and it was drawn and rejected. It would give
 * every linked bib the SAME accessible name — one string, "View on Strava", repeated across
 * as many races as the wall holds, which is the SC 2.4.4 failure this file otherwise works to
 * avoid — while shrinking a whole bib to a 24px row on races that have nothing wrong with
 * them. (No count here on purpose: how many bibs link is a property of the data on the day
 * you read this, and the argument does not depend on it.) Only the exception pays, which is
 * the rule `booked_label` is already decided by a few paragraphs up: you mark the exception,
 * not the norm.
 *
 * `target="_blank"` and NO `rel`, matching this site's existing convention — Now.astro and
 * IntroCard.astro both use a bare `target="_blank"` and browsers imply `noopener`.
 * Introducing `rel` on one link out of three is how a convention starts to look accidental.
 *
 * NO `aria-label`. The accessible name is the bib's whole text — READ OFF CHROME'S
 * ACCESSIBILITY TREE, not assembled by hand, because this comment got it wrong the first time
 * and no assertion could see that (the guard uses `toContain`, which is order-blind by
 * construction). The name is verbose and is a true superset of what is on screen, this repo's
 * rule; an aria-label would REPLACE all of it with a summary.
 *
 * THE ACTION ROW MOVED THE CALL TO ACTION FROM THIRD PLACE TO LAST, which is the position it
 * should always have had. It used to sit in the meta row as an `sr-only` transcription of the
 * glyph, so a listener heard "12 JUL 2026 RIDE ON STRAVA 158.10 KM …" — the destination
 * announced between the sport and the distance, before the race had even been named. Now the
 * words are visible, they are the last thing on the bib, and the announcement follows the
 * reading order:
 *
 *     "12 JUL 2026 RIDE 158.10 KM MBG DCR 2026 - KRABI TO PHUKET THAILAND
 *      ELAPSED 9:41:31 VIEW ON STRAVA"
 *
 * That is a side effect of a change made for sighted readers, and it is the better ordering on
 * its own terms. Re-read it off the AX tree rather than trusting this paragraph if the row ever
 * moves again — the last revision of this comment was falsified by exactly that.
 */
const date = patchDateSegments(event);
const goal = goalForSport(event.sport);
const [whole, fraction] = event.km.toFixed(2).split(".");
const recordings = recordingsOf(event);
// The ONE recording a bib can be the anchor for, or null where there is none or several.
// Everything downstream keys on this rather than counting again, so the three cases cannot
// drift apart: no recordings (a plain bib), one (today's bib, unchanged), many (the stub).
const only = recordings.length === 1 ? recordings[0] : null;
const parts = recordings.length > 1 ? recordings : null;
// `div` rather than `li`: the LIST ITEM is the cell wrapper now, and a second one nested
// inside it is invalid HTML that no test in this repo reads markup strictly enough to see.
// Caught by looking at the built page, which is the only place it showed.
const Tag = only === null ? "div" : "a";
---

<li class="bib-cell">
<Tag
        class:list={["bib", `bib--${event.sport}`, state === "booked" && "bib--booked",
                     only !== null && "bib--linked", parts !== null && "bib--split"]}
        href={only === null ? undefined : stravaActivityUrl(only)}
        target={only === null ? undefined : "_blank"}
>
    <p class="bib-meta">
        {/* One <time> per endpoint, never one around the whole range: `datetime` names a
            single instant, so a tour wrapped in one would tell a machine that nine days
            of riding happened on the 7th. The dash carries no date and is plain text. */}
        {date && (
                <span class="bib-date">
                    {date.map((seg) => seg.iso ? <time datetime={seg.iso}>{seg.text}</time> : seg.text)}
                </span>
        )}
        <span class="bib-sport">
            <span class={`${iconClass(goal.goal_logo)} bib-icon`} aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <span class="bib-word">{goal.short_name}</span>
        </span>
        {state === "booked" && <span class="bib-tag">{PATCHES.booked_label}</span>}
    </p>
    <p class="bib-value">{whole}<span class="bib-fraction">.{fraction}</span></p>
    <p class="bib-unit">{goal.measurable_unit}</p>
    <p class="bib-name">{event.name}</p>
    <p class="bib-place">{event.country}</p>
    {event.elapsed_time && (
            <p class="bib-time">
                <span class="bib-time-label">{PATCHES.elapsed_label}</span>
                <span class="bib-time-value">{event.elapsed_time}</span>
            </p>
    )}
    {only !== null && (
            <p class="bib-go">
                <span class={`${iconClass(PATCHES.strava_icon)} bib-icon bib-go-mark`} aria-hidden="true"></span>
                {PATCHES.strava_name}
            </p>
    )}
    {/* THE STUB, WHERE A RACE WAS RECORDED IN PARTS. One line per recording, each its own
        link, each printing the distance and the clock waiting at the other end of it — so
        the bib's summed hero is never what any link promises.

        THE LINES CARRY FIGURES AND NO WORDS, which is what lets this scale. A shared label
        repeated once per recording is information at two parts and noise at four, the same
        argument `booked_label` is decided by. Figures do not go stale that way, and a run of
        them is a splits table, which is what a results sheet prints anyway.

        The destination and the race are said in the accessible name instead — this bib's
        rule is that the name is a true superset of what is on screen, never an aria-label
        replacing it, and a reader listing every link on the page needs both. */}
    {parts !== null && (
            <ul class="bib-splits">
                {parts.map((part) => (
                        <li>
                            <a class="bib-split" href={stravaActivityUrl(part)} target="_blank">
                                <span class={`${iconClass(PATCHES.strava_icon)} bib-icon bib-go-mark`} aria-hidden="true"></span>
                                <span class="bib-split-km">{PATCHES.split_line.replace("{distance}", `${part.km.toFixed(2)} ${goal.measurable_unit}`)}</span>
                                {" "}
                                <span class="bib-split-time">{part.elapsed_time}</span>
                                <span class="sr-only">{" " + PATCHES.split_name.replace("{race}", event.name) + " "}</span>
                                <span class="sr-only">{NEW_TAB_NOTICE}</span>
                            </a>
                        </li>
                ))}
            </ul>
    )}
    {/* THE NOTICE FOLLOWS THE ACTION ROW, and the order is the point: the announced name now ends
        with the call to action and then the warning, so the warning qualifies the control it
        belongs to instead of trailing the elapsed time. Both are conditional on the LINK rather
        than on the state — an earned bib with no Strava id is an ordinary <div> and opens nothing.

        (The obvious phrasing of what NEW_TAB_NOTICE says is not written here on purpose: that
        word is a UnoCSS utility, and prose in an `.astro` file is extracted like markup, so
        naming it emits a rule no element wears and the orphan gate fails the build.)

        The measurement in NEW_TAB_NOTICE was taken when this name was 92 characters and the
        destination was announced THIRD, inside the meta row. The action row moved that to last and
        the name is now 97 characters plus this notice; re-read it off the AX tree rather than
        trusting either figure. */}
    {only !== null && <span class="sr-only">{NEW_TAB_NOTICE}</span>}
</Tag>
</li>

<style>
    /*
     * THE GRID ITEM IS THE CELL, THE BIB IS INSIDE IT. One wrapper, for one reason: where
     * a race has a verified activity the bib itself is an anchor, and an anchor cannot be
     * a child of `.patch-wall` — that is a list and its children are list items.
     *
     * `display: grid` here rather than nothing, so the bib fills a cell the wall's row has
     * stretched. Without it a bib in a row with a taller neighbour sits at its own height
     * and the row goes ragged, which is a defect the previous single-element structure got
     * for free and this one has to ask for.
     *
     * The cell carries no treatment of its own. Every class-based selector this file and
     * tests/patch-wall.test.ts use still lands on `.bib`, so the structure changed and the
     * colour model did not.
     */
    .bib-cell {
        display: grid;
    }

    /*
     * THE BIB'S OWN BOX. Four locals do all the work of the two treatments, so a
     * treatment is a set of values rather than a second copy of the layout:
     *
     *   --face   the bib's background
     *   --hole   the pin holes punched out of it
     *   --ink    everything printed on it
     *   --sport  the sport mark, which is the one thing that is not --ink
     *
     * A FINISHED BIB IS INVERTED — face --text, ink --background — which is the
     * strongest mark this palette can make and is spent here deliberately: an earned
     * bib is the thing the page is about. A booked bib is the same box with the face
     * removed. Nothing about the two treatments differs except these values and the
     * border, which is why they cannot drift apart in layout.
     */
    .bib {
        --face: var(--text);
        --hole: var(--card-background);
        --ink: var(--background);
        /* Finished: the mark sits on the inverted face, so it takes the ink-side hue.
           `--sport-on-ink` is set by the per-sport rule below, on this same element —
           a custom property resolves against the element's own computed value, so the
           order these two rules appear in cannot matter. */
        --sport: var(--sport-on-ink);
        container-type: inline-size;
        display: grid;
        grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) auto;
        grid-template-areas: "date date" "value unit" "name name" "place place" "time time";
        align-content: start;
        row-gap: 0.3rem;
        padding: 0.7rem 0.8rem 0.65rem;
        border: 1px solid transparent;
        border-radius: 2px;
        color: var(--ink);
        background-color: var(--face);
        /*
         * FOUR PIN HOLES FOR A RUN, TWO FOR A RIDE, and the count is the information.
         * A running bib is pinned at four corners; a ride's frame number is zip-tied
         * at two. It reads as a detail and it is a second, non-colour channel for the
         * sport — the icon and the word being the first two.
         *
         * Drawn as radial gradients rather than as pseudo-elements because there are
         * up to four of them and one background-image is one declaration. 1.7px solid
         * to 1.8px transparent is the antialiasing band; a hard stop renders a stepped
         * circle at this size.
         */
        background-image:
            radial-gradient(circle at 7px 7px, var(--hole) 0 1.7px, transparent 1.8px),
            radial-gradient(circle at calc(100% - 7px) 7px, var(--hole) 0 1.7px, transparent 1.8px),
            radial-gradient(circle at 7px calc(100% - 7px), var(--hole) 0 1.7px, transparent 1.8px),
            radial-gradient(circle at calc(100% - 7px) calc(100% - 7px), var(--hole) 0 1.7px, transparent 1.8px);
    }

    .bib--cycling {
        --sport-on-card: var(--sport-ride);
        --sport-on-ink: var(--sport-ride-on-ink);
        background-image:
            radial-gradient(circle at 7px 7px, var(--hole) 0 1.7px, transparent 1.8px),
            radial-gradient(circle at calc(100% - 7px) 7px, var(--hole) 0 1.7px, transparent 1.8px);
    }

    .bib--running {
        --sport-on-card: var(--sport-run);
        --sport-on-ink: var(--sport-run-on-ink);
    }

    /*
     * BOOKED: A BIB YOU HAVE NOT EARNED YET. No face — so no holes either, because a
     * hole is punched out of something — and normal-polarity ink on the card.
     *
     * The holes are removed explicitly rather than left to disappear on their own.
     * They would: --hole is --card-background and the face is transparent, so they
     * paint card-on-card and vanish. Note the hazard that is NOT the reason — an
     * earlier revision of this comment claimed re-toning --card-background would put
     * grey dots on a booked bib, and it cannot: the hole and the surface behind the
     * bib are the SAME token, so they re-tone together. The real reason is that the
     * coincidence holds only while a bib sits on a card. Put one on any other surface
     * — the page background, a tinted panel — and four dots appear on a bib that is
     * meant to have none, with nothing in the source saying they were ever drawn.
     *
     * The mark now sits on the CARD, so it takes the card-side hue. This is the swap
     * the token names in BasicLayout.astro exist to make legible; getting it backwards
     * is how a mark ends up at 2.77:1 with every structural assertion still green.
     */
    .bib--booked {
        --face: transparent;
        --ink: var(--text);
        --sport: var(--sport-on-card);
        border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--text) 32%, transparent);
        background-image: none;
    }

    /*
     * FORCED COLOURS FLATTEN THE TWO TREATMENTS INTO ONE. The whole distinction is
     * carried by background-color and border-color, and a forced-colours mode
     * overrides both — a finished bib loses its face, a booked bib's hairline becomes
     * the same CanvasText as everything else, and the wall becomes six identical
     * boxes. The tag still says "Booked", so no information is lost, but the shape
     * stops helping.
     *
     * A thicker border on the earned bib restores the distinction in the one channel
     * that survives: border-style and border-width are not overridden. This is a
     * different mark from the one sighted readers get, which is the correct trade —
     * matching the inverted face is impossible here, and a distinction is worth more
     * than a faithful one that cannot be drawn.
     */
    @media (forced-colors: active) {
        .bib {
            border-color: CanvasText;
            border-width: 3px;
        }

        .bib--booked {
            border-width: 1px;
        }

        /* The mode discards --accent along with everything else, so the outline would vanish
           and a linked bib would be indistinguishable from an unlinked one in the only
           channel it had left. LinkText is the colour reserved for exactly this, and it
           is a system colour rather than a token, so it is set here. */
        .bib--linked {
            border-color: LinkText;
        }

    }

    /*
     * PRINT IS THE SAME PROBLEM AS FORCED COLOURS, so it gets the same answer.
     *
     * A finished bib is legible on screen because its ink is `--background` on a
     * `--text` face. Print drops the face — Chrome's default `print-color-adjust:
     * economy` discards backgrounds and keeps the ink — and what is left is the pale
     * half of the palette on white paper. A review of the wall measured the printed
     * result at 2.43:1, under the 4.5:1 floor, and in the dark theme that ink is
     * #FAFAFA, which is nothing at all.
     *
     * So every bib prints as an outline and the earned one keeps its distinction in the
     * channel that survives, which is exactly the trade the forced-colours rules above
     * makes. LITERAL BLACK RATHER THAN A TOKEN is deliberate: paper is white whichever
     * theme the reader had on screen, so every token here carries an assumption that is
     * only true half the time. The sport mark goes black with the rest — its hue is one
     * of three channels naming the sport and the icon and the word are the other two,
     * both of which print.
     */
    @media print {
        .bib {
            --face: transparent;
            --hole: transparent;
            --ink: #000;
            --sport: #000;
            border-color: #000;
            border-width: 2px;
            background-image: none;
        }

        .bib--booked {
            border-width: 1px;
        }
    }

    .bib-meta {
        grid-area: date;
        display: flex;
        flex-wrap: wrap;
        align-items: center;
        gap: 0.45em;
        margin: 0;
        font-size: 0.625rem;
        font-weight: 700;
        letter-spacing: 0.14em;
        text-transform: uppercase;
    }

    /*
     * THE DATE AND THE TAG ARE DIMMED; THE SPORT MARK IS NOT. That split is the whole
     * reason these are three elements rather than one row with an opacity on it.
     *
     * The row carried `opacity: .8` as a unit in the design rig, and an ancestor
     * opacity composites everything under it — including a sport colour that was
     * chosen against the face it is now no longer quite sitting on. Measured on the
     * rendered pixel, that alone took the finished ride mark from #F3A3AA to
     * rgb(196,132,138), 9.96:1 down to 6.57:1, on a bib where nothing was supposed to
     * be dimmed at all. getComputedStyle cannot see it: it reports the authored
     * colour, which ancestor opacity does not touch.
     *
     * So the dimming is scoped to the two things that are meant to be quiet, and the
     * mark keeps the hue it was measured as.
     */
    .bib-date,
    .bib-tag,
    .bib-time-label {
        opacity: 0.8;
    }

    /*
     * THE THIRD UNBREAKABLE TOKEN ON A BIB, AND IT WAS MISSED TWICE. `.bib-place` and
     * `.bib-time` were each given this after escaping the bib; "Booked" is the same shape —
     * one word with nowhere to break — and nothing had measured it.
     *
     * Found on a sweep of ORIGIN/MAIN taken as the baseline for this change, so it predates
     * everything here: measured against the bib's border box at 320px wide, the tag escapes by
     * 31.45px at a 44px root and 63.11px at 48. Outside the WCAG 1.4.4 bracket (which tops out
     * at 32) and therefore not a conformance failure, which is presumably why two passes over
     * this file walked past it — but the ink lands on the card at 1.045:1, so the word carrying
     * a bib's STATE is unreadable there, and that state has no other text carrier.
     *
     * tests/patch-wall.test.ts now holds all four rows to this rule rather than the two that
     * had already failed, which is the difference between a gate on a class and a gate on the
     * instances someone happened to notice.
     */
    .bib-tag {
        overflow-wrap: anywhere;
    }

    .bib-sport {
        display: inline-flex;
        align-items: center;
        gap: 0.35em;
        color: var(--sport);
    }

    /* Not shrink-0 as a utility: this is scoped CSS and the icon is a flex item whose
       artwork is 1em square. Letting it compress deforms the glyph, which is a defect
       this project has already shipped once.
       Its own class rather than `.bib-sport > span:first-child`, for two reasons: the
       positional selector silently moves to the word if anything is ever inserted
       before the icon, and a combinator here is unrepresentable in the class-subset
       colour model that tests/patch-wall.test.ts uses — which now refuses such rules
       rather than mis-reading them. */
    .bib-icon {
        flex: none;
    }

    .bib-word {
        font-weight: 800;
    }

    /*
     * THE DISTANCE IS THE HERO, and it is sized against the BIB rather than against
     * the viewport: `22cqi` reads the bib's own inline size, so what the number is
     * proportional to is the box it is printed on and not the window.
     *
     * WHICH OF THE TWO TERMS ACTUALLY BINDS — measured, because an earlier revision of
     * this comment guessed and guessed wrong. It advertised a phone's one-column bib
     * and "a four-column wall on a desktop" printing at the same proportion; neither
     * half survives measurement.
     *
     *   at a 16px root   the 3rem cap binds at every width from 320 to 3840 except one
     *                    (768, where 22cqi resolves to 47.96 against a 48px cap), so
     *                    the hero is a constant 48px across the whole desktop range
     *   at a 42px root   22cqi binds on a narrow bib — 33px on the 150px-wide bib at
     *                    320px — and the number shrinks with its box instead of
     *                    overflowing it
     *   columns          the wall reaches THREE, never four: `main` is `max-w-4xl`, so
     *                    the grid stops widening long before a fourth 13rem track fits
     *
     * So the container query is not what varies the hero across viewports; the cap does
     * that, and it does it by holding still. The cqi term earns its place under TEXT
     * ZOOM, where a rem-only size would keep growing while the bib does not. Both are
     * needed and each covers what the other cannot: the cap keeps 1022.00 — seven
     * characters, the widest the calendar holds — from filling a 13rem column at the
     * default size, and the cqi term keeps it inside a bib the reader's own text size
     * has squeezed to 150px.
     */
    .bib-value {
        grid-area: value;
        margin: 0;
        font-size: min(3rem, 22cqi);
        font-weight: 800;
        line-height: 0.82;
        letter-spacing: -0.045em;
    }

    .bib-fraction {
        font-size: 0.4em;
        padding-inline-start: 0.08em;
    }

    /*
     * Set on its side, the way a bib prints its unit down the edge of the number.
     * `rotate(180deg)` puts the baseline on the correct side of a vertical-rl run.
     *
     * THE OPACITY IS 0.7 BECAUSE 0.55 FAILED, and the number is a floor rather than a
     * taste: this line arrived from the design rig at .55, where the ink composites
     * to 4.29:1 on a booked bib in the light theme and 4.11:1 on a finished one in
     * the dark. Both are under the 4.5:1 a 10px word needs, and neither can be found
     * by anything that reads the authored colour — the ink is `--background` on
     * `--text`, which is 18.86:1 before the opacity touches it.
     *
     * At 0.7 the same two land at 7.28:1 and 6.92:1, computed from the composite, and
     * 7.40 / 7.03 sampled from the rendered pixel. Quote the computed pair: the
     * sampler reads an extreme pixel of a 10px rotated glyph, so it is the noisier of
     * the two instruments here even though it is the authoritative one for the
     * question of WHETHER anything is compositing.
     *
     * The clearance is worth keeping: the composite runs against the FACE, and both
     * faces are near the extremes of the palette, so any future re-tone moves this
     * ratio. tests/patch-wall.test.ts composites every dimmed line on the bib and
     * holds all of them to the text floor, in both states and both themes.
     */
    .bib-unit {
        grid-area: unit;
        align-self: end;
        margin: 0;
        writing-mode: vertical-rl;
        transform: rotate(180deg);
        font-size: 0.625rem;
        font-weight: 800;
        letter-spacing: 0.2em;
        text-transform: uppercase;
        opacity: 0.7;
    }

    /*
     * WHERE THE RACE IS, on its own line under the name rather than appended to it.
     *
     * The name row already wraps to two lines on the longest event the calendar holds, so
     * a middot and a country on the same line would make the wrap point depend on the
     * length of two unrelated strings. Its own row costs 15px flat on a page that
     * scrolls — /patches has no height clamp, unlike the home page — and it is one grid
     * area rather than a nested box.
     *
     * It is dimmed with the date and the tag rather than set at full ink: the place is
     * context for the race, not a second name for it. That puts it under the same
     * composited-contrast assertion as those two, which is where the 4.5:1 floor is
     * actually checked — the authored ink is 18.86:1 and tells you nothing.
     */
    .bib-place {
        grid-area: place;
        margin: 0;
        overflow-wrap: anywhere;
        font-size: 0.625rem;
        font-weight: 700;
        letter-spacing: 0.14em;
        text-transform: uppercase;
        opacity: 0.8;
    }

    /*
     * A LINKED BIB SAYS SO IN WORDS, NOT WITH A HOVER. There is no hover on a phone, and this
     * site has already removed one card hover for advertising an affordance that did not
     * exist — the opposite failure is an affordance that exists and is never advertised.
     * The action row at the foot of the bib is permanent and is TEXT, so it carries the fact
     * without leaning on colour (SC 1.4.1).
     *
     * That used to read "the Strava mark in the meta row", which described a 7.5x10px glyph
     * that no longer exists there — see `.bib-go`. The mark was the whole cue and was not
     * enough; the words are.
     *
     * The hover and focus outline is the second cue rather than the only one. It is an
     * OUTLINE rather than the border, and that is what makes its contrast answerable: an
     * outline paints outside the bib, so the surface it must clear is the card, which is
     * the pair `--accent` is already held to 3:1 against in tests/build-output.test.ts.
     * The border would have painted against the bib's own face, which differs by state
     * and by theme — four pairs to hold instead of one.
     *
     * THE TWO STATES ARE SPLIT ACROSS TWO RULES NOW, WHICH IS NOT A STYLE CHOICE. A touch
     * browser applies `:hover` on tap and holds it until the reader taps elsewhere, so on a
     * phone this outline stayed on whichever bib was last pressed, reading as a selected
     * state the bib does not have. Every `hover:` UTILITY on the site is guarded by a variant in uno.config.ts;
     * a hand-written rule has to carry the guard itself, and it cannot be applied to the
     * selector list as a whole because `:focus-visible` is a KEYBOARD indicator that must
     * survive on every device. tests/build-output.test.ts fails the build on any `:hover`
     * rule that ships outside a `(hover: hover)` context.
     */
    .bib {
        text-decoration: none;
    }

    .bib--linked:focus-visible {
        outline: 2px solid var(--accent);
        outline-offset: 2px;
    }

    @media (hover: hover) {
        .bib--linked:hover {
            outline: 2px solid var(--accent);
            outline-offset: 2px;
        }
    }

    /*
     * AND A PRESS HAS TO SHOW SOMETHING, WHICH IS A DEBT THE GUARD ABOVE CREATED.
     *
     * Guarding the outline was right, but it was the only thing this bib did when a finger
     * landed on it. UnoCSS's preflight sets `-webkit-tap-highlight-color: transparent` on
     * `html, :host`, so the platform's own press flash is off site-wide — which means that
     * after the guard, a tap on a bib painted NOTHING until the next page rendered.
     *
     * The site already answers this everywhere else: the shared plate idiom in uno.config.ts
     * carries its press affordance on `:active`, which is not hover-gated and does still fire
     * on touch. So
     * without this the two objects on this site that say "the whole box is the anchor" — the
     * goal card's call to action and the bib — disagree about whether a press is acknowledged.
     * This is the bib saying the same thing in the bib's own idiom.
     *
     * `:active` is the correct state and not a workaround: it is defined for the interval
     * between press and release on every input, so it costs a pointer reader nothing and is
     * the only affordance a touch reader can produce deliberately.
     */
    .bib--linked:active {
        outline: 2px solid var(--accent);
        outline-offset: 2px;
    }

    /*
     * THE ACTION ROW: WHAT THE BIB DOES, SAID IN WORDS.
     *
     * It replaces a 7.5 x 10px Strava glyph in the meta row whose only text was `sr-only` —
     * 75px² on a 324 x 141px bib, 0.16% of it, monochrome and unlabelled. That was the whole of
     * what told a reader a bib could be clicked, and two friends reviewing the site did not see
     * it. The row answers both halves of what they reported: it says the bib is a control and
     * what using it does, and because ONLY a bib with a recording carries the row, it is also
     * what distinguishes the bibs that link from the ones that do not.
     *
     * FULL INK, AND EMPHATIC WEIGHT, DELIBERATELY. Every other quiet line on this bib — the
     * date, the booked tag, the elapsed label, the place — shares `opacity: 0.8`, and dropping
     * this row into that group would draw the one line that is a control exactly like the four
     * that are captions. It is the second thing on the bib set at full strength, after the
     * distance. Keeping it out of the dimmed set also keeps it out of the composited-contrast
     * sweep's dimmed group, where its authored 18.86:1 is the honest number rather than one
     * that has to be re-derived through an alpha.
     *
     * `flex-wrap` and `overflow-wrap` for the reason `.bib-time` has them and the same trap:
     * "Strava" is a single unbreakable token, the bib does NOT clip, and ink that escapes a bib
     * paints onto the card at 1.045:1 — unreadable rather than absent, and unseen by any sweep
     * that walks only clipping ancestors.
     *
     * (The first draft of this comment used the obvious adjective for that, twice, and turned
     * the orphan gate red: the word is a UnoCSS utility, and prose in an `.astro` file is
     * extracted like markup. Same footgun `underline` is blocklisted for, one word further on.)
     */
    .bib-go {
        grid-area: go;
        display: flex;
        flex-wrap: wrap;
        overflow-wrap: anywhere;
        align-items: center;
        gap: 0.4em;
        margin: 0.25rem 0 0;
        padding-top: 0.5rem;
        font-size: 0.625rem;
        font-weight: 800;
        letter-spacing: 0.14em;
        text-transform: uppercase;
        /*
         * THE PERFORATION IS THIS ROW'S OWN BACKGROUND, NOT A PSEUDO-ELEMENT, and four separate
         * gates in this repo are why. It was built as `::before` first, and that version tripped
         * every one of them at once:
         *
         *   the bib's colour resolver (patch-wall.test.ts) refuses any rule reaching a bib
         *   through a combinator or pseudo, because it models the cascade as a class subset and
         *   would otherwise read the rule as applying when it does not;
         *   the absolute-height gate, since a 1px pseudo needs a pinned `height`;
         *   the every-class-has-a-rule gate, once the label's own rule went away with the
         *   text decoration; and this file's own not-dimmed assertion, whose selector matched `::before`
         *   and read the perforation's alpha as the ROW being dimmed.
         *
         * Painting it here needs no box, no height and no opacity: the alpha lives in the colour
         * via `color-mix`, which is how `.bib--booked` already tones its border, and the dots sit
         * on the row's own padding. One rule, one class, and every gate satisfied by construction
         * rather than by exemption.
         */
        background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 1.5px 0.5px,
            color-mix(in srgb, currentColor 45%, transparent) 0 1px, transparent 1.2px);
        background-size: 6px 1px;
        background-repeat: repeat-x;
        background-position: top left;
    }

    /*
     * THE TEAR-OFF PERFORATION ABOVE IS THE ONE PIECE OF THIS CHANGE THAT IS NOT A FIX.
     *
     * A race bib is perforated above the stub that carries your result — the part you hand in,
     * or the part you keep. What sits below that line is where this ride's result actually
     * lives, so the device is not decoration: it is the bib's own material vocabulary saying
     * the same thing the words say. That is the whole argument for it, and if the row ever
     * stops leading somewhere the perforation has to go with it.
     *
     * IT BORROWS THE PIN-HOLES' CONSTRUCTION BUT NOT THEIR GEOMETRY OR THEIR TOKEN, and both
     * departures were measured rather than reasoned.
     *
     * The obvious move is to draw a perforation exactly like a corner pin-hole — same
     * `radial-gradient`, same 1.7px-to-1.8px antialiasing band, same `--hole` token, on the
     * argument that a perforation and a pin-hole are the same event on real paper. It was built
     * that way first and rendered wrong twice over. At 1.7px on a 6px tile the dots very nearly
     * touch, so a RUN of them reads as a heavy dashed rule rather than as holes — a pin-hole
     * gets its character from being isolated. And `--hole` at full strength is the card colour
     * against an inverted face, the strongest mark this palette can make; on a row meant to be
     * a quiet piece of stationery it shouted.
     *
     * So: 1px dots on the same 6px tile, in the row's own ink at 45%. Fine enough to read as
     * perforation, quiet enough to stay behind the words it introduces. `currentColor` rather
     * than a token means it follows the row into forced colours without naming anything.
     */

    /*
     * NO TEXT DECORATION HERE, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE OF WHY THIS ROW LOOKS THE WAY IT DOES.
     *
     * A first revision drew this label the way the three text links elsewhere on the site are
     * drawn. It was wrong twice over, and both reasons are worth keeping because the mistake is
     * an easy one to repeat:
     *
     *   IT IS THE WRONG VOCABULARY. A bib is a printed artifact. Every row on it is uppercase,
     *   letterspaced and undecorated, and `.bib` sets `text-decoration: none` on the anchor
     *   deliberately. A ruled link is a WEB convention imported into a paper one, and it read as
     *   exactly that — the one element on the bib that did not belong to the bib.
     *
     *   IT DESCRIBED THE WRONG TARGET. The whole bib is the anchor, ~260px of it. A decoration
     *   under 15px of ink advertises those 15px as the control, so it does not merely fail to
     *   help — it tells the reader something false about where to aim.
     *
     * What carries the affordance instead is all bib-native: an imperative verb phrase that says
     * what using it does, the brand glyph beside it, and FULL INK at the bib's emphatic weight
     * against four dimmed captions. On this bib only the distance is louder.
     */
    /*
     * THE ROW IS DECLARED ON THE LINKED BIB, NOT ON `.bib`, and that is a height decision rather
     * than tidiness. `.bib` carries ONE shared `grid-template-areas` with a 0.3rem `row-gap`, so
     * adding a sixth area there would charge every bib on the wall for a row that only two of
     * them render — and `.bib-cell { display: grid }` stretches a bib to its row's height, so
     * the cost would propagate to each linked bib's row-mates as well.
     */
    .bib--linked,
    .bib--split {
        grid-template-areas: "date date" "value unit" "name name" "place place" "time time" "go go";
    }

    /*
     * THE STUB IS PERFORATED ONCE, NOT ONCE PER LINE, and that is the difference between
     * material vocabulary and decoration. A race bib has A stub — it does not sprout a second
     * one because a watch stopped — so the perforation stays on the LIST and the recordings
     * are printed on it. A device that scales with the length of an array has stopped
     * describing the object and started describing the data.
     *
     * Same construction as `.bib-go`, which is the row this replaces on a split bib: 1px dots
     * on a 6px tile, in the row's own ink at 45%, painted on the element's own padding rather
     * than by a pseudo-element. The four gates that forbid the pseudo are written out above
     * `.bib-go`; they apply here identically.
     */
    .bib-splits {
        grid-area: go;
        display: grid;
        gap: 0;
        margin: 0.25rem 0 0;
        padding-top: 0.5rem;
        padding-inline: 0;
        padding-bottom: 0;
        list-style: none;
        background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 1.5px 0.5px,
            color-mix(in srgb, currentColor 45%, transparent) 0 1px, transparent 1.2px);
        background-size: 6px 1px;
        background-repeat: repeat-x;
        background-position: top left;
    }

    /*
     * ONE RECORDING, AS A SPLIT LINE. Its own distance and its own clock, which are exactly
     * the two figures a reader would otherwise be ambushed by at the other end of the link.
     *
     * `min-height` IS THE TARGET FLOOR AND IT IS NOT DECORATIVE. This row is a target where
     * the whole bib used to be one. Without the declaration it renders 24.59px against
     * SC 2.5.8's 24px — a 0.6px pass resting on UnoCSS preflight's `line-height: 1.5`, which
     * nothing here declares or asserts; at 1.4 it fails. And two split lines are vertically
     * adjacent with no gap, so the spacing exception is arithmetically identical to the size
     * rule (centre-to-centre distance IS the row height) and can never rescue one. 1.5rem is
     * the floor this repo already sets for a small control, and being font-relative it scales
     * with the reader's text instead of staying put.
     *
     * THE INLINE PADDING IS FOR THE FOCUS OUTLINE, and the negative margin undoes it for the
     * ink. An inset outline with no padding paints exactly on the leading edge of the
     * content — through the Strava mark — and on the trailing edge through the clock. The
     * padding gives the outline somewhere to land; the margin pulls the BOX back out so the
     * figures still line up with the name, the place and the elapsed row above them. The
     * perforation is on the list rather than this row, so it keeps the content width and
     * does not widen with the padding.
     *
     * `flex-wrap` and `overflow-wrap` for the reason `.bib-time` and `.bib-go` have them,
     * and the same trap: a bib does NOT clip, so ink that escapes paints onto the card at
     * 1.045:1 — unreadable rather than absent, and unseen by any sweep that walks only
     * clipping ancestors. `17.90` and `1:28:41` are unbreakable tokens of the same shape as
     * the elapsed row's, which was measured escaping from a 42px root.
     */
    .bib-split {
        display: flex;
        flex-wrap: wrap;
        overflow-wrap: anywhere;
        align-items: center;
        gap: 0.4em;
        margin-top: 0;
        margin-bottom: 0;
        margin-inline: -0.35rem;
        padding-top: 0.15rem;
        padding-bottom: 0.15rem;
        padding-inline: 0.35rem;
        min-height: 1.5rem;
        color: inherit;
        opacity: 0.8;
        text-decoration: none;
        font-size: 0.625rem;
        font-weight: 800;
        letter-spacing: 0.14em;
        text-transform: uppercase;
    }

    .bib-split-km {
        font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    }

    /*
     * The clock is dimmed and the distance is not, which is the same split `.bib-time` makes
     * one row up: the distance is what tells one link from another, the clock is context for
     * it. That puts this under the composited-contrast sweep with the date, the tag and the
     * place, where the floor is actually checkable — the authored ink is `--background` on
     * `--text`, 18.86:1, and tells you nothing.
     */
    .bib-split-time {
        margin-left: auto;
        font-weight: 700;
        font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
        letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    }

    /*
     * THE STATE IS AN OUTLINE BECAUSE NOTHING ELSE IS ALLOWED TO BE ONE HERE, and it is drawn
     * in the bib's own ink rather than in `--accent`. Both halves are measured.
     *
     * The bib's own outline paints OUTSIDE the bib, so the surface it must clear is the card
     * — the pair `--accent` is already held to 3:1 against, at 6.52:1 light and 12.55:1 dark.
     * A split line sits INSIDE the inverted face, where that same token lands at 2.77:1 and
     * 1.37:1, both under SC 1.4.11. (2.77 is the number the booked-bib note above already
     * flags for the sport mark: the palette has one accent and it was chosen against the
     * card.) `currentColor` is `--ink` on `--face`, 18.86:1 by construction, and it follows
     * the treatment per state and per theme instead of needing four pairs re-derived.
     *
     * The bib-native move — inverting the line the way the sport chips draw the current page
     * — is not available: patch-wall.test.ts refuses any selector carrying a pseudo-class on
     * a rule declaring colour, background colour, opacity or a custom property, so that would
     * be a red build on correct code. `outline` is not a modelled property, which is what
     * makes it the one channel a state has inside a bib.
     *
     * OFFSET INWARDS, because two split lines are adjacent with no gap and an outset outline
     * would paint into the next target.
     *
     * The hover half is split from the others for the reason `.bib--linked`'s is: a touch
     * browser applies `:hover` on tap and holds it, while `:focus-visible` is a keyboard
     * indicator every device needs. `:active` is not gated either, and is the only affordance
     * a touch reader can produce deliberately — it matters more here than on the bib, because
     * the platform's own tap flash is `--accent` at 18% and composites to 1.09:1 on an
     * inverted face, which is nothing at all.
     */
    .bib-split:focus-visible {
        outline: 2px solid currentColor;
        outline-offset: -1px;
    }

    .bib-split:active {
        outline: 2px solid currentColor;
        outline-offset: -1px;
    }

    @media (hover: hover) {
        .bib-split:hover {
            outline: 2px solid currentColor;
            outline-offset: -1px;
        }
    }

    /*
     * THE TIME IS LABELLED, and the label is the point rather than a caption. Elapsed and
     * moving are far apart on a long ride — 8:32:05 against 5:03:55 on the 10 July ride —
     * so a bare time invites a reader to divide it into the distance printed above it and
     * conclude one speed where the rider held another. What makes the division HONEST is
     * that both figures come off the same scope: on a single-recording bib that is the
     * activity, and on a split bib it is the race, summed distance over the whole span. What
     * it must never be is a mixture — a summed distance over one part's clock, which is the
     * 18.8 the old event-scope distance produced and belonged to nothing. The label names
     * which clock so the reader does not have to guess which. See `km` and `elapsed_time` in
     * constants.ts, and `Recording` for why each split line prints its own pair.
     *
     * The label is dimmed with the date, the tag and the place, which puts it under the
     * same composited-contrast assertion as those three: the authored ink is `--background`
     * on `--text`, 18.86:1 before any opacity touches it, so the floor is only checkable
     * from the composite. The VALUE is not dimmed — it is the second real figure on the
     * bib after the distance, and the only one that says what the day cost.
     */
    .bib-time {
        grid-area: time;
        display: flex;
        /*
         * IT WRAPS, AND IT BREAKS. Two separate defects, and the first fix did not cover the
         * second — a review panel found that and the numbers here are the corrected ones.
         *
         * `flex-wrap` splits the row into two lines. That is necessary and not sufficient:
         * each line is then sized independently, and `ELAPSED` and `9:41:31` are single
         * unbreakable tokens, so at a large enough text size each one is individually wider
         * than the bib and paints OUTSIDE it. Measured against the bib's BORDER box (the
         * meaningful edge — the 0.8rem of padding inside it is inverted face, where ink is
         * perfectly legible) at 320px: the label escapes from a 40px root (+9.88px) and the
         * value from 42px (+1.55px), reaching +75.5 and +47.11 at a 48px root. There the ink
         * is `--background` on the CARD rather than on the bib's face — 1.045:1, unreadable —
         * so "9:41:31" reads as "9:41:" and "ELAPSED" as "ELAP".
         *
         * `overflow-wrap: anywhere` is what fixes it, and `anywhere` rather than `break-word`
         * for the reason recorded on the wall's page heading: `break-word` does not affect intrinsic
         * minimum sizing, so it breaks the rendered line and leaves the overflow. `.bib-place`
         * takes it too — "SINGAPORE" and "THAILAND" are the same unbreakable-token case and
         * were escaping EARLIER and further than the time (+28.92px at a 40px root, +98.3 at
         * 48) since before this row existed. Verified after: every measured escape is negative
         * at every configuration, i.e. inside the border box.
         *
         * TWO INSTRUMENTS MISSED THIS AND THE REASON IS THE SAME. The wall sweep walks only
         * elements inside a clipping ancestor, and `.bib` does not clip
         * (`container-type` is not a clip) — so the ink escapes onto the card and the sweep
         * reports nothing. Before that it excluded no visually-suppressed subtree either, and the
         * sr-only text this bib introduced buried the real loss under thousands of pixels of
         * fake one. A page that does not clip needs its own edge measured.
         */
        flex-wrap: wrap;
        overflow-wrap: anywhere;
        align-items: baseline;
        gap: 0.45em;
        margin: 0;
        font-size: 0.625rem;
        letter-spacing: 0.14em;
        text-transform: uppercase;
    }

    .bib-time-label {
        font-weight: 700;
    }

    .bib-time-value {
        font-weight: 800;
        font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
        letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    }

    .bib-name {
        grid-area: name;
        margin: 0;
        font-size: 0.6875rem;
        font-weight: 600;
        letter-spacing: 0.05em;
        line-height: 1.3;
        text-transform: uppercase;
        overflow-wrap: break-word;
    }

    /*
     * ==========================================================================================
     * MODE OVERRIDES. EVERYTHING BELOW THIS LINE MUST STAY BELOW EVERY PLAIN RULE ABOVE IT.
     * ==========================================================================================
     *
     * An at-rule adds NO specificity, so `@media {...}` and a plain rule are the same weight and
     * the later one wins. Two overrides in this file were once written up beside their same-mode
     * siblings and were dead from the moment they were written — forced colours kept painting an
     * `--accent` outline the mode discards, and print kept a hover outline on paper.
     *
     * THE NOTE THAT RECORDED THAT WAS ITSELF STALE, AND I REPRODUCED THE BUG BEHIND IT. It said
     * these blocks sat "at the foot of the sheet"; they did not — `.bib-time`, `.bib-time-label`,
     * `.bib-time-value` and `.bib-name` were all below them, which was harmless only because
     * none of those four happened to touch a property an override sets. Adding the action row
     * broke that coincidence: its base `.bib-go` rule landed below the print at-rule, and the
     * print override measured DEAD in the built stylesheet — offset 3680 against a base rule at
     * 3955. Caught by reading emitted rule offsets out of `dist`, which is the only place source
     * order is observable; nothing in the suite and nothing in review saw it.
     *
     * So the blocks were moved to where the comment always claimed they were. Anything
     * mode-specific belongs here, at the actual end, and any new plain rule belongs ABOVE.
     */
    /*
     * THE HOVER HALF OF EACH OVERRIDE BELOW CARRIES `and (hover: hover)`, matching the base
     * rule it overrides. Neither could misfire on a phone without it — one paints only in
     * forced colours and the other only on paper — but the gate in tests/build-output.test.ts
     * is a universal with no carve-outs, and a universal is worth more than two exemptions
     * that each need re-arguing. Note `(hover: hover)` is TRUE while printing from a pointer
     * device (measured, not assumed), so the print override is still live where it matters.
     */
    @media (forced-colors: active) {
        .bib--linked:focus-visible {
            outline-color: LinkText;
        }
    }

    @media (forced-colors: active) and (hover: hover) {
        .bib--linked:hover {
            outline-color: LinkText;
        }
    }

    @media (forced-colors: active) {

        /*
         * The mark is an icon class, so its artwork is a MASK over
         * `background-color: currentColor` — and background-color is a forced property, so
         * without this the glyph paints Canvas on Canvas and vanishes. Measured on the previous
         * revision with the mode emulated: glyph rgb(255,255,255) on a bib face of
         * rgb(255,255,255), a ratio of exactly 1. Same fix as EventsLink.astro's chevron.
         *
         * IT MOVED HERE WITH THE GLYPH rather than being deleted alongside the row that used to
         * carry it. What it protects is smaller than it was — the row's WORDS survive forced
         * colours whatever happens to the glyph, so the label is no longer identified by a mark
         * that can disappear — but a brand mark that paints nothing is still a defect, and this
         * is one declaration.
         */
        .bib-go-mark {
            forced-color-adjust: none;
            background-color: LinkText;
        }

        /*
         * `LinkText` on the row, so it is coloured as a link rather than as body text — and in
         * this mode that colour is doing real work, because the row carries no text decoration
         * (see the plain rule above for why a rule on a bib is the wrong vocabulary). The words
         * and the perforation both survive; this is what makes them read as a control.
         */
        .bib-go {
            color: LinkText;
        }

        /*
         * THE PERFORATION IS A BACKGROUND IMAGE, WHICH THIS MODE DISCARDS, so it is redrawn in
         * the one channel forced colours does not touch: border-style and border-width. Same
         * trade the two bib treatments make a few rules up — a distinction drawn differently is
         * worth more than a faithful one that cannot be drawn at all.
         *
         * The dots become dashes, which is the closest a border can come to a row of holes. It
         * is not the pin-holes' language any more, but nothing in this mode is: the corner holes
         * disappear here too, for exactly the same reason.
         */
        .bib-go {
            background-image: none;
            border-top: 1px dashed CanvasText;
        }

        /* The split lines take the same three treatments the action row does, for the same
           three reasons: `LinkText` so they read as controls in a mode that discards the
           text decoration they never had, and the perforation redrawn as a dashed border
           because a background image is exactly what this mode throws away. */
        .bib-split {
            color: LinkText;
        }

        .bib-splits {
            background-image: none;
            border-top: 1px dashed CanvasText;
        }
    }

    @media (forced-colors: active) and (hover: hover) {
        .bib-split:hover {
            outline-color: LinkText;
